  "executive_summary": "This study investigates the multi-step magnetization process in the Shastry-Sutherland lattice compound TbB4 using resonant magnetic x-ray diffraction (RMXD) under strong pulsed magnetic fields up to 29 T. While TbB4 possesses an XY-type ground state, which typically predicts a linear magnetization response, experiments reveal a complex multi-step metamagnetic behavior above 16 T, including a prominent one-half magnetization plateau. The authors demonstrate that these plateau phases are not purely Ising-like (collinear) but contain significant in-plane (XY) magnetic moment components, contradicting the standard expectation that plateau phases consist solely of moments aligned with the field. By analyzing the polarization dependence of the diffraction intensity, specifically the 100 reflection, the study confirms the coexistence of hard XY-type moments and Ising-like excited states. A simplified spin model incorporating both XY ground states and Ising excited states with an energy gap is proposed. Calculations using this model successfully reproduce the one-half magnetization plateau and suggest that geometric frustration inherent to the Shastry-Sutherland lattice lifts the degeneracy at critical fields, thereby driving the successive metamagnetic transitions. The work highlights that frustration can both create and break degeneracy, offering a new mechanism for understanding multi-step magnetization in frustrated magnets. The integration of high-field pulsed magnet technology with third-generation synchrotron RMXD is emphasized as a powerful tool for probing high-field magnetic structures.",
